+# Design: fix audit for the spec-panel review loop (`/sc:spec-panel-ponytail`, issue 346)
+
+Status: built as an additive variant; not routed and not adopted elsewhere. The full design is `.dev/brainstorms/346-ponytail/SPEC.md`; this note keeps its decisions.
+
+## What it is
+
+One fix audit per review loop, at the end of fix cycle 2: after the reviser applies the cycle-2 fixes and before review 3. It runs as its own step, `/sc:spec-panel-ponytail @<spec> --fix-audit --re-review <review-2 report> --baseline <copy review 2 saw>`, in a fresh session that is neither the reviser nor the review-3 reviewer. That session invokes `ponytail:ponytail-review` with a fixed brief and writes a per-fix table; its brief forbids it to edit the document, and check K1 fails the audit if the document changed. The standard-library script `fix_audit.py` (in the `sc-fix-audit` skill) checks the table and makes every edit to the specification in one atomic write (a temp file, then `os.replace`). Review 3 then runs as today on the audited document, and after review 3 the same script computes the open blocking list and the verdict from files. The audit runs once and nobody re-grades its table; the only text it can add back is text review 2 already read.
+
+## The loop, step by step
+
+| # | Step | Actor | Reads | Writes |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Review 1 (`/sc:spec-panel`, unchanged) | reviewer | spec | review-1 report |
+| 2 | Fix cycle 1 (unchanged) | reviser | review-1 report | spec, disposition |
+| 3 | Coordinator saves the copy review 2 will see (unchanged) | coordinator | spec | `history/spec-v2` |
+| 4 | Review 2 (`--re-review <R1> --baseline <v1>`, unchanged) | reviewer | spec, R1, v1 | review-2 report `review: 2 of 3` |
+| 5 | Fix cycle 2 (unchanged) | reviser | review-2 report | spec, `disposition-round2.md` |
+| 6 | Fix audit (new): `/sc:spec-panel-ponytail @<spec> --fix-audit --re-review <R2> --baseline <v2>` | audit session | R2, v2, spec, disposition-round2 | `fix-audit/` beside R2; the spec (by script only) |
+| 7 | Review 3 (`--re-review <R2> --baseline <v2>`, unchanged) | reviewer (another session) | audited spec, R2, v2 | review-3 report |
+| 8 | Verdict (new): `fix_audit.py verdict --dir <D> --report <R3>` | coordinator (a script call) | R3, `fix-audit/` | `fix-audit/final-verdict.md` |
+| 9 | Close on PASS; on FAIL escalate with the open blocking list (unchanged) | coordinator | final-verdict | none |
+
+Step 6 acts only when a line of the review-2 report starts with `review: 2 of 3`, its last non-empty line is `verdict: FAIL`, the baseline-to-spec diff (whitespace-only lines ignored) is not empty, and both files are readable; otherwise it records `audit: not-triggered(<reason>)` in `fix-audit/state.json`, exits 0 and changes nothing.
+
+## Safe apply
+
+- `fix_audit.py apply` is the step that edits the specification. The auditor brief allows the auditor to write only `fix-audit/audit.md`, and check K1 fails the audit if the spec's sha256 changed since `prepare`. Invoking `--fix-audit` is the consent for that one scripted edit; the pre-audit copy is kept as `fix-audit/current-before-audit.md`, so undoing the audit is one copy.
+- A revert is a hunk id: the script restores the hunk's baseline lines itself. A hunk is reverted only if every id on it has verdict revert; otherwise `REVERT_SHARED <id>` is logged and its text stays.
+- Edit rows follow the exact-once old/new pattern: `delete` rows may remove only words the fix added and must keep every finding id of the hunk; `restore` rows only in a minor-only hunk, and only the aligned baseline span.
+- A finding review 2 graded blocking, or protected (CRITICAL, FR-8, FR-9), is never closed by the audit. A validated-minor grade on an id review 2 graded blocking is logged `DOWNGRADE <id>` only; a protected id that review 2 did not grade blocking gets no `DOWNGRADE` line.
+
+## How the verdict is computed
+
+`fix_audit.py verdict --dir <D> --report <R3> [--report-rule 427|any-severity]` reads review 3's resolution table and new findings with the same tolerant parse as `prepare`. Open = prior findings review 2 graded blocking that review 3 lists `open` or `regressed`, plus new findings graded blocking (an unknown grade counts as blocking). A closed id that review 3 grades blocking prints `consistency: DISAGREE` and forces FAIL; an unparseable table exits 2 with `verdict: FAIL`. The script never turns a FAIL into a PASS. In `any-severity` mode (the S1 setting) the report's own line is printed as `t09-verdict:`.
+
+## Owner count
+
+Owner decision: findings that the script defers (reverted, listed in `closed-minor.txt`) or demotes (logged `DOWNGRADE`) stay open in the success measure. `final-verdict.md` prints `held open (deferred or demoted): ...`, `review-3 new critical+major: <n>` and `review-3 critical+major (owner count, held findings included): <n>`, which adds each held id that review 2 labelled CRITICAL or MAJOR, unless review 3 already counts that id as a new critical or major finding. Nothing in the script lowers the owner count.
+
+## Placement
+
+Recommendation: placement P (end of fix cycle 2, before review 3, own step). It is the owner's design, the only one that runs once and can still move the registered review-3 count, and the cheapest. Running the audit after every fix cycle is predicted to be at most 0.1 better at twice the cost and is pre-registered only as a follow-up. P is predicted to land on the review-3 bar, not clearly under it, so it is not expected to produce a clean PASS.
+
+## Owner decisions applied
+
+1. The success measure counts findings the script defers, demotes or withdraws as still open (the owner count above).
+2. The S1 review-3 brief (T09) is not edited, including its "any finding of any severity is FAIL" line.
+3. The `--fix-audit` flag in the brief is the explicit consent for the one scripted edit or revert.
+4. The audit runs after the cycle-2 fixes and before review 3 (placement P).
+
+## Variant base and the Unattended-mode deviation
+
+The variant is a byte copy of the 472-line Arm B file `spec-panel-post427-95f2c6e4.md` (sha256 `c8d80346a96cb528fce6cff3bcadfed5bc95d1eee48dea0ac275404314b8df2f`; copied as `tests/skills/fixtures/spec-panel-post427-base.md`) plus the `## Fix Audit` section, the `--fix-audit` Usage line and the renamed frontmatter, H1 and Usage line. That base is the current `spec-panel.md` without its `## Unattended mode` section, its `--unattended` Usage line and its `--target-release` sentence. The variant omits `## Unattended mode` because the earlier experiment arms ran the 472-line file without it, the S1 briefs never pass `--unattended`, and a copy that kept the section would fail `tests/skills/test_worker_contract.py` and make the worker-contract brief guard refuse the T08a command line. So `--unattended` is not supported by the variant. The original `src/superclaude/commands/spec-panel.md` is not changed.
+
